 **Passing tracks.** A station with [passing tracks](station-upgrades.md) changes who waits. The slow train that stops there waits for the fast ones to pass, and the fast line keeps its speed. A slow train leaves 60 seconds after the last fast train has passed. It waits no longer than the gap between two fast trains, since the next one would catch it just the same, and never so long that it holds up its own next train. Several passing stations in a row pass trains the same way, as often as each line's headways make them meet. Each slow train has its own times, so it waits only for the fast trains that would catch it, and one that no fast train catches does not wait at all. Before the first passing station there is nowhere to step aside, so a fast train that catches a slow one on the way there follows it until the station. On weekends a line with its own weekend trains runs the average waits.
 
-**Averages in the figures.** The round trip, the trains a line needs and the trip times riders see use the average wait and the average loss per train. The trains on the map and in the timetable chart run their own times. Where a slow line's own waits one way add up to more than its trains have to spare at the end, that direction runs the average waits. A fast train slows behind a slow one only as much as it has to spare at the end, so it still makes its next departure. Past that it catches the slow train and waits behind it on the map.
+**Averages in the figures.** The round trip, the trains a line needs and the trip times riders see use the average wait and the average loss per train. The trains on the map and in the timetable chart run their own times. On a line that keeps a headway, where one slow train's own waits make it too late for its next departure, all its trains stand a headway longer at that end, and the line runs a train more. Where a slow line's own waits one way add up to more than its trains have to spare at the end, that direction runs the average waits. A fast train slows behind a slow one only as much as it has to spare at the end, so it still makes its next departure. Past that it catches the slow train and waits behind it on the map.
 
 The loss counts like any other delay: it lengthens the round trip, so the line needs more trains for the same headway, and the average speed falls. It shows up in the trip times riders see ([perceived minutes](perceived-minutes.md)). It does not fill track, so it is not a place trains queue ([line capacity](line-capacity.md)).
 
